[{"date":"2015-06-04","post_id":1235960,"title":"NGC 2419: Intergalactic Wanderer","permalink":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-4-ngc-2419-intergalactic-wanderer/","media_type":"image","explanation":"<strong>Explanation:</strong> Three objects stand out in <a href=\"http://bf-astro.com/ngc2419/ngc2419.htm\" target=\"_blank\">this thoughtful telescopic image</a> , a view toward the mostly stealthy constellation <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynx_%28constellation%29\" target=\"_blank\">Lynx</a> . The two brightest (the spiky ones) are nearby stars. The third is the remote globular star cluster <a href=\"http://messier.seds.org/xtra/ngc/n2419.html\" target=\"_blank\">NGC 2419</a> , at distance of nearly 300,000 light-years. <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/0712.0729\" target=\"_blank\">NGC 2419</a> is sometimes called \u201cthe Intergalactic Wanderer\u201d, an appropriate title considering that the distance to the Milky Way's satellite galaxy, the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap130528.html\">Large Magellanic Cloud</a> , is only about 160,000 light-years. Roughly similar to other large globular star clusters like <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap140529.html\">Omega Centauri</a> , NGC 2419 is itself intrinsically bright, but appears faint because it is so far away. <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/1310.4499\" target=\"_blank\">NGC 2419</a> may really have an extragalactic origin as, for example, the remains of a small galaxy <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap080619.html\">captured and disrupted</a> by the Milky Way. But its extreme distance makes it difficult to <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9707048\" target=\"_blank\">study</a> and compare its properties with other globular clusters that roam the halo of <a href=\"http://csep10.phys.utk.edu/astr162/lect/milkyway/ components.html\" target=\"_blank\">our Milky Way galaxy</a> .","credit":"<b> Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/about_apod.html#srapply\">Copyright</a>: </b> <a href=\"http://bf-astro.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Bob Franke</a>","copyright":"<b> Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/about_apod.html#srapply\">Copyright</a>: </b> <a href=\"http://bf-astro.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Bob Franke</a>","alt":"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-4-ngc-2419-intergalactic-wanderer/","hdurl":"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/ngc2419Frankebig.jpg?w=1500&h=1000&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint","basic_html":"<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2015 June 4 - NGC 2419: Intergalactic Wanderer</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-4-ngc-2419-intergalactic-wanderer/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Three objects stand out in this thoughtful telescopic image , a view toward the mostly stealthy constellation Lynx . The two brightest (the spiky ones) are nearby stars. The third is the remote globular star cluster NGC 2419 , at distance of nearly 300,000 light-years. NGC 2419 is sometimes called \u201cthe Intergalactic Wanderer\u201d, an appropriate title considering that the distance to the Milky Way&#039;s satellite galaxy, the Large Magellanic Cloud , is only about 160,000 light-years. Roughly similar to other large globular star clusters like Omega Centauri , NGC 2419 is itself intrinsically bright, but appears faint because it is so far away. NGC 2419 may really have an extragalactic origin as, for example, the remains of a small galaxy captured and disrupted by the Milky Way. But its extreme distance makes it difficult to study and compare its properties with other globular clusters that roam the halo of our Milky Way galaxy .\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"ngc 2419: intergalactic wanderer\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"NGC 2419: Intergalactic Wanderer\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-4-ngc-2419-intergalactic-wanderer/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/ngc2419Frankebig.jpg?w=1500&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/ngc2419Frankebig.jpg?w=1500&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2015-06-04T00:00:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-07-29T13:44:37-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"NGC 2419: Intergalactic Wanderer\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/ngc2419Frankebig.jpg?w=1500&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2015 June 4<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/ngc2419Frankebig.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/ngc2419Frankebig.jpg\" alt=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> NGC 2419: Intergalactic Wanderer </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<b> Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/about_apod.html#srapply\">Copyright</a>: </b> <a href=\"http://bf-astro.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Bob Franke</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Three objects stand out in <a href=\"http://bf-astro.com/ngc2419/ngc2419.htm\" target=\"_blank\">this thoughtful telescopic image</a> , a view toward the mostly stealthy constellation <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynx_%28constellation%29\" target=\"_blank\">Lynx</a> . The two brightest (the spiky ones) are nearby stars. The third is the remote globular star cluster <a href=\"http://messier.seds.org/xtra/ngc/n2419.html\" target=\"_blank\">NGC 2419</a> , at distance of nearly 300,000 light-years. <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/0712.0729\" target=\"_blank\">NGC 2419</a> is sometimes called \u201cthe Intergalactic Wanderer\u201d, an appropriate title considering that the distance to the Milky Way's satellite galaxy, the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap130528.html\">Large Magellanic Cloud</a> , is only about 160,000 light-years. Roughly similar to other large globular star clusters like <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap140529.html\">Omega Centauri</a> , NGC 2419 is itself intrinsically bright, but appears faint because it is so far away. <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/1310.4499\" target=\"_blank\">NGC 2419</a> may really have an extragalactic origin as, for example, the remains of a small galaxy <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap080619.html\">captured and disrupted</a> by the Milky Way. But its extreme distance makes it difficult to <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9707048\" target=\"_blank\">study</a> and compare its properties with other globular clusters that roam the halo of <a href=\"http://csep10.phys.utk.edu/astr162/lect/milkyway/ components.html\" target=\"_blank\">our Milky Way galaxy</a> .\n<p> <center>\n\n<b> Tomorrow's picture: </b><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-5-green-flash-at-moonrise/\">APOD: 2015 June 5 &#8211; Green Flash at Moonrise</a>\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-3-flyby-image-of-saturns-sponge-moon-hyperion/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-5-green-flash-at-moonrise/\">&gt;</a>\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\n<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/department/faculty/nemiroff/\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Nemiroff</a> (<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/\" target=\"_blank\">MTU</a>) &amp; <a href=\"https://inspirehep.net/authors/1073087\" target=\"_blank\">Jerry Bonnell</a> (<a href=\"https://www.astro.umd.edu/\" target=\"_blank\">UMCP</a>)<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>","basic_html_url":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/150604/html"},{"date":"2015-06-03","post_id":1235956,"title":"Flyby Image of Saturn's Sponge Moon Hyperion","permalink":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-3-flyby-image-of-saturns-sponge-moon-hyperion/","media_type":"image","explanation":"<strong>Explanation:</strong> Why does this moon look like a sponge? To better investigate, <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a> and <a href=\"http://www.esa.int/\" target=\"_blank\">ESA</a> sent the Saturn-orbiting robotic <a href=\"http://www.esa.int/Our_Activities/Space_Science/Cassini-Huygens/Cassini_spacecraft\" target=\"_blank\">spacecraft Cassini</a> zooming past <a href=\"http://solarsystem.nasa.gov/planets/profile.cfm?Object=Saturn\" target=\"_blank\">Saturn</a> 's moon <a href=\"http://solarsystem.nasa.gov/planets/profile.cfm?Object=Hyperion\" target=\"_blank\">Hyperion</a> , once again, earlier this week. One of the images beamed back to Earth is featured above , raw and unprocessed. Visible, as expected, are many <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap130630.html\">unusually shaped craters</a> with an unusual dark material at the bottom. Although <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperion_(moon)\" target=\"_blank\">Hyperion</a> spans about 250 kilometers, its small gravitational tug on <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/cassini/main/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">Cassini</a> indicates that it is mostly empty space and so has very low <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surface_gravity\" target=\"_blank\">surface gravity</a> . Therefore, the <a href=\"http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2012Icar..220..268H\" target=\"_blank\">odd shapes of many of Hyperion's craters</a> are thought to result from impacts that primarily compress and eject surface material -- instead of the more <a href=\"http://curious.astro.cornell.edu/our-solar-system/38-our-solar-system/the-earth/impacts/47-why-are-craters-round-beginner\" target=\"_blank\">typical round craters</a> that appear after a circular shock wave that explosively redistributes surface material. Cassini is <a href=\"http://saturn.jpl.nasa.gov/mission/saturntourdates/\" target=\"_blank\">on track</a> for another flyby of <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fAQM9rfZq7w\" target=\"_blank\">Saturn</a> 's <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap121105.html\">Dione</a> in about two weeks.<br /><br /><b> Retrospective: </b> <a href=\"http://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?f=46&amp;t=28711\" target=\"_blank\">All Previous June 3 APODs</a> <br />","credit":"<b> Image Credit: </b> <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a> , <a href=\"http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">JPL-Caltech</a> , <a href=\"http://ciclops.org/\" target=\"_blank\">SSI</a>","copyright":"<b> Image Credit: </b> <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a> , <a href=\"http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">JPL-Caltech</a> , <a href=\"http://ciclops.org/\" target=\"_blank\">SSI</a>","alt":"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-3-flyby-image-of-saturns-sponge-moon-hyperion/","hdurl":"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/hyperion02_cassini_1024.jpg?w=1024&h=1024&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint","basic_html":"<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2015 June 3 - Flyby Image of Saturn&#039;s Sponge Moon Hyperion</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-3-flyby-image-of-saturns-sponge-moon-hyperion/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Why does this moon look like a sponge? To better investigate, NASA and ESA sent the Saturn-orbiting robotic spacecraft Cassini zooming past Saturn &#039;s moon Hyperion , once again, earlier this week. One of the images beamed back to Earth is featured above , raw and unprocessed. Visible, as expected, are many unusually shaped craters with an unusual dark material at the bottom. Although Hyperion spans about 250 kilometers, its small gravitational tug on Cassini indicates that it is mostly empty space and so has very low surface gravity . Therefore, the odd shapes of many of Hyperion&#039;s craters are thought to result from impacts that primarily compress and eject surface material -- instead of the more typical round craters that appear after a circular shock wave that explosively redistributes surface material. Cassini is on track for another flyby of Saturn &#039;s Dione in about two weeks. Retrospective:  All Previous June 3 APODs\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"flyby image of saturn&#039;s sponge moon hyperion\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Flyby Image of Saturn&#039;s Sponge Moon Hyperion\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-3-flyby-image-of-saturns-sponge-moon-hyperion/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/hyperion02_cassini_1024.jpg?w=1024&#038;h=1024&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/hyperion02_cassini_1024.jpg?w=1024&#038;h=1024&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2015-06-03T00:00:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-07-29T13:44:37-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Flyby Image of Saturn&#039;s Sponge Moon Hyperion\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/hyperion02_cassini_1024.jpg?w=1024&#038;h=1024&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2015 June 3<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/hyperion02_cassini_1024.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/june/hyperion02_cassini_1024.jpg\" alt=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Flyby Image of Saturn&#039;s Sponge Moon Hyperion </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<b> Image Credit: </b> <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a> , <a href=\"http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">JPL-Caltech</a> , <a href=\"http://ciclops.org/\" target=\"_blank\">SSI</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Why does this moon look like a sponge? To better investigate, <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a> and <a href=\"http://www.esa.int/\" target=\"_blank\">ESA</a> sent the Saturn-orbiting robotic <a href=\"http://www.esa.int/Our_Activities/Space_Science/Cassini-Huygens/Cassini_spacecraft\" target=\"_blank\">spacecraft Cassini</a> zooming past <a href=\"http://solarsystem.nasa.gov/planets/profile.cfm?Object=Saturn\" target=\"_blank\">Saturn</a> 's moon <a href=\"http://solarsystem.nasa.gov/planets/profile.cfm?Object=Hyperion\" target=\"_blank\">Hyperion</a> , once again, earlier this week. One of the images beamed back to Earth is featured above , raw and unprocessed. Visible, as expected, are many <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap130630.html\">unusually shaped craters</a> with an unusual dark material at the bottom. Although <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperion_(moon)\" target=\"_blank\">Hyperion</a> spans about 250 kilometers, its small gravitational tug on <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/cassini/main/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">Cassini</a> indicates that it is mostly empty space and so has very low <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surface_gravity\" target=\"_blank\">surface gravity</a> . Therefore, the <a href=\"http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2012Icar..220..268H\" target=\"_blank\">odd shapes of many of Hyperion's craters</a> are thought to result from impacts that primarily compress and eject surface material -- instead of the more <a href=\"http://curious.astro.cornell.edu/our-solar-system/38-our-solar-system/the-earth/impacts/47-why-are-craters-round-beginner\" target=\"_blank\">typical round craters</a> that appear after a circular shock wave that explosively redistributes surface material. Cassini is <a href=\"http://saturn.jpl.nasa.gov/mission/saturntourdates/\" target=\"_blank\">on track</a> for another flyby of <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fAQM9rfZq7w\" target=\"_blank\">Saturn</a> 's <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap121105.html\">Dione</a> in about two weeks.<br /><br /><b> Retrospective: </b> <a href=\"http://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?f=46&amp;t=28711\" target=\"_blank\">All Previous June 3 APODs</a> <br />\n<p> <center>\n\n<b> Tomorrow's picture: </b><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-4-ngc-2419-intergalactic-wanderer/\">APOD: 2015 June 4 &#8211; NGC 2419: Intergalactic Wanderer</a>\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-2-polaris-and-comet-lovejoy/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-4-ngc-2419-intergalactic-wanderer/\">&gt;</a>\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\n<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/department/faculty/nemiroff/\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Nemiroff</a> (<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/\" target=\"_blank\">MTU</a>) &amp; <a href=\"https://inspirehep.net/authors/1073087\" target=\"_blank\">Jerry Bonnell</a> (<a href=\"https://www.astro.umd.edu/\" target=\"_blank\">UMCP</a>)<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>","basic_html_url":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/150603/html"}]